{"language_code":"eng","language_probability":1.0,"text":"Everyone knows Pandora's Box, but here's the twist. In the original Greek myth, there was no box at all. It was a jar. The story comes from the poet Hesiod, about 700 years before Christ. It starts with a theft. The Titan Prometheus stole fire from the gods and gave it to humans. Zeus was furious, so as a punishment, he ordered the gods to create the first woman, Pandora. According to the myth, Hephaestus, the god of the forge, shaped her out of earth and water. Athena taught her weaving, Aphrodite gave her beauty, and Hermes gave her a clever tongue and a sly mind. Her name means all gifts. Zeus sent her as a gift to Epimetheus, the brother of Prometheus, even though Prometheus had warned him, \"Never accept a gift from Zeus.\" In his house stood a large sealed jar, and inside it, says the myth, were all the evils of the world: sickness, hard work, and grief. Then Pandora lifted the lid. Hesiod never says why. The curiosity part? That was added later. Out flew all the evils and spread across the world. She slammed the lid shut, but only Hope was left inside, stuck under the rim. Why Hope stayed in the jar is still debated. Was it a comfort or just one more trick? [laughs] So how did the jar become a box? In 1508, the scholar Erasmus translated the Greek word pithos, a big jar, into the Latin word for box, and that little mistake stuck for 500 years. So if you like history and myths where even the famous version is wrong, follow for more stories like this.","words":[{"text":"Everyone","start":0.12,"end":0.56,"type":"word","logprob":-0.0004654039512388408},{"text":" ","start":0.56,"end":0.6,"type":"spacing","logprob":-9.536738616588991e-7},{"text":"knows","start":0.6,"end":0.84,"type":"word","logprob":-9.536738616588991e-7},{"text":" ","start":0.84,"end":0.96,"type":"spacing","logprob":-0.00002288792165927589},{"text":"Pandora's","start":0.96,"end":1.54,"type":"word","logprob":-0.000676542834196899},{"text":" 